<p>Facebook's iOS app doesn't have a built-in "save video" button, so you need to grab the video's link and resolve it through a tool like this one. Here's the full process, plus what to do when it doesn't work the first time.</p>

<h2>Step 1: Open the video and get its share link</h2>
<p>In the Facebook app, tap the <strong>Share</strong> icon below the video (the arrow icon), then choose <strong>Copy Link</strong>. If you're watching in Safari instead of the app, tap the three-dot menu on the video and choose <strong>Copy link</strong> from there.</p>
<p>Reels use a slightly different flow — see our <a href="/guides/facebook-reels-downloader-guide.php">Reels downloader guide</a> if the link you copied starts with <code>facebook.com/reel/</code>.</p>

<h2>Step 2: Paste the link into <?= e(SITE_NAME) ?></h2>
<p>Open Safari or Chrome on your iPhone, go to the <?= e(SITE_NAME) ?> homepage, and paste the link into the input field at the top. Tap <strong>Download</strong>. You don't need to log in or install anything.</p>

<h2>Step 3: Choose a quality and download</h2>
<p>Once the video resolves, you'll see a thumbnail, the title, and one or more quality buttons (HD and/or SD, depending on what Facebook makes available for that video). Tap the quality you want.</p>

<h2>Step 4: Save it to your camera roll</h2>
<p>iOS downloads through Safari go to the <strong>Files</strong> app by default, not directly to Photos. To move it to your camera roll:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Open the <strong>Files</strong> app and go to <strong>Downloads</strong>.</li>
  <li>Tap and hold the video file, then choose <strong>Share</strong>.</li>
  <li>Select <strong>Save Video</strong> to add it to your Photos library.</li>
</ul>
<p>If you're using Chrome on iOS, downloaded files also land in the Files app under Downloads — the process is identical from there.</p>

<h2>Common iPhone-specific issues</h2>
<p><strong>The download doesn't start.</strong> Safari sometimes blocks automatic downloads from pop-up-style triggers. Make sure you tapped the quality button directly rather than through a redirected link, and check Safari's download settings under Settings → Safari → Downloads.</p>
<p><strong>"Private or requires login" error.</strong> This means the video isn't public — friends-only posts, group videos, and videos on private profiles can't be resolved by any third-party tool, including this one, since that would require your Facebook login. Ask the poster to make it public first.</p>
<p>For anything else, see our full <a href="/guides/fix-facebook-video-download-errors.php">troubleshooting guide</a>.</p>
